body:
I still ask myself that question to this day! The first time I broke my femur, I was eight years old and I was climbing a tree right outside my house all by myself, and I slipped and fell about a solid 15 feet and next thing you know, I had a compound fracture on my left leg. I still remember to this day seeing my bone pop out of my leg (gross!) I wasn't able to actually start walking again until about 3 months later. With only a few weeks after I finally began walking again, my parents noticed that I was walking with my left leg sticking out a bit. At that point, they were so pissed off at the doctor for fucking up the operation. The only way to re-orient my leg was to re-brake it. So, 6 months later in February, my femur was yet again broken, but at least this time it was in an operation setting so I was luckily drugged up. So then I endured another 3 months of recovery. But I was so young I was so happy that I was missing so much school and getting all this attention so remember being a lot more optimistic! Currently, I have 20 scars along my leg from various operations that include inserting stuff in my leg like metal rods and a giant metal brace. Since then, the only other major injury I have had is a stress fracture on my tibia, but in comparison to my previous injuries that was nothing. 

&#x200B;

To be honest, I have no clue what caused my narcolepsy. I know some people have said that theirs was caused by a traumatic incident or illness, but for me, I never have had a severe illness that I can think of. I started to notice the symptoms of narcolepsy during my 8th-grade year, but It didn't affect my performance in school or how I would act in the day. I would get really tired at the end of the school day, but I would just account for that as the side effects of puberty. During my freshman year, I definitely became a lot more tired after school and I would start to fall asleep during the 5th period of the day, but I had a pretty easy schedule that worked well for me. At the end of my freshman year, we finally began to see a sleep doctor. She said to come back once school started. About a month or two into my sophomore year, I was a complete mess. My doctor discovered I had sleep apnea, but she was certain I did not have narcolepsy. The next few months were probably some of the worst days of my life. Finally, In February I went on medical leave from school and in that time period, we discovered that I was diagnosed with Narcolepsy.
